--TEST--
A construct the front end accepts and nothing below it could act on is refused at its own site — a placement no core answers to is `E0818` (`rule:concurrency/on-worker-runs-the-child-on-another-core`) — while `require` is whole in both of `rule:statements/require-is-the-only-inclusion-construct`'s forms and reports nothing at either
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:statements/a-require-expression-is-mixed`'s value form runs now: the site calls the target file's own
// script frame and keeps what its file-scope `return` handed back, typed
// `mixed` because it cannot be known statically. `nvs-ir`'s known gap 22 is
// closed and `E0704` is retired with it — no diagnostic below names this
// line, which is what this case still asserts about it.
mixed $c = require "cfg.nvs";
echo $c as string;

// The statement form is the same call with its value discarded, and the
// class the target declares is reachable from here either way: declarations
// cross a `require` (§ *Decision*).
require "cfg.nvs";
echo Cfg::name();

// `rule:security/isolate-shares-nothing`'s spawn itself lowers now (`E0703` is retired), and all
// five of its options are checked. What is refused is a placement that names
// no core: `on:` takes one of two written words, because which scheduler
// starts the child is decided here and a spelling nobody declared has no
// answer at run time but a spawn that fails.
spawn script "cfg.nvs" with(on: "remote");
--FILE cfg.nvs--
<?nvs
class Cfg {
    public static function name(): string {
        return "cfg";
    }
}
return "cfg-value";
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0818]: `remote` is not a placement
   --> %s:21:33
   |
21 | spawn script "cfg.nvs" with(on: "remote");
   |                                 ^^^^^^^^ no core is named this
   = help: `on: "worker"` starts the child on another core and `on: "here"` starts it on this one, which is what a spawn without the option does. A placement a program computes is two spawns under an `if`

error: aborting due to 1 error
